<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- The Finder extension, unlike the app around it, is sandboxed — and
         sandboxed with nothing else granted.

         It can afford to be. Finder hands it the selection over its own
         channel, as URLs; the extension reads the path out of each one and
         writes a string to the pasteboard. It opens no file, launches nothing,
         and talks to no network, so every hole the app has to keep open —
         microphone, calendars, Apple events, executable memory for MLX — would
         be dead weight here.

         Worth keeping that way. This process is loaded into Finder's context
         on every right-click, which makes it the widest-reaching piece of
         Cyclop and the one with the least to do.

         Reading paths needs no file-access entitlement: `selectedItemURLs`
         returns URLs, and a path is a string. The first thing that tries to
         open one of those files will need `files.user-selected.read-only` and
         should think twice before adding it. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
